<p>If you searched for this because your cat just took a bite out of your spider plant, here is the short version: spider plant is not on the ASPCA's toxic list for cats. It is one of the most commonly suspected houseplants, probably because it is also one of the most common houseplants, period, and because cats seem to love chewing on its long, grassy leaves. But "not toxic" is not quite the same as "do whatever you want." A cat who eats a lot of spider plant can still end up with a sour stomach, and there are other plants sitting on windowsills in the same house that are genuinely dangerous, one of them seriously enough to kill a cat within days. This is a plant-by-plant breakdown of what's actually safe, what's actually not, and where the real emergency lives.</p>

<h2>Spider Plant: The Myth, Corrected</h2>

<p>Spider plant (<em>Chlorophytum comosum</em>) appears on the ASPCA's non-toxic list for cats, dogs, and horses alike. There's no poison here. What there is, is a plant that acts a bit like grass: cats are drawn to chewing it, and eating enough of any plant material, toxic or not, can irritate the digestive tract. So a cat that's been nibbling a spider plant may vomit, get mild diarrhea, or seem a little off for a day, not because of a toxin, but because they overdid it on roughage. Some people have also pointed to a mildly hallucinogenic compound in spider plant that seems to make cats a little "extra" after chewing it, similar to catnip, though this isn't something the ASPCA lists as a safety concern.</p>

<p>The practical takeaway: you don't need to get rid of a spider plant over this. If your cat treats it like a salad bar daily, moving it somewhere less accessible is reasonable, but it's not a plant you need to panic over, and it's genuinely one of the better houseplant choices for a cat household precisely because the worst-case outcome is a stomachache.</p>

<h2>The Plant That Deserves Real Fear: True Lilies</h2>

<p>None of the other plants on this list come close to the danger of a true lily. <em>Lilium</em> species (Easter lilies, tiger lilies, Asiatic and Oriental lilies) and daylilies (<em>Hemerocallis</em>) are capable of causing acute kidney failure in cats, and it doesn't take much. The FDA has stated plainly that every part of these plants is toxic to cats, including the pollen and even the water sitting in a vase. A cat that grooms a dusting of pollen off its fur, or drinks from a vase holding a few lily stems, can be poisoned.</p>

<p>Early signs show up within hours: vomiting, lethargy, drooling, and a loss of appetite. The dangerous part is what's happening internally even when a cat looks like it's recovering, because kidney damage can progress over the following one to three days. By the time a cat is clearly very sick, treatment windows start closing, and veterinary sources note that damage is often permanent if treatment is delayed much past 18 hours. There is no home remedy for this. If a cat has any contact with a lily, including just being in a room with a bouquet, the right move is a vet visit immediately, not a wait-and-see approach, even if the cat seems completely fine at the time.</p>

<p>This site has a separate deep dive on holiday flowers if lilies are turning up around Christmas or Easter arrangements: see our post on <a href="/blog/are-poinsettias-toxic-to-cats-and-dogs/">whether poinsettias are toxic to cats and dogs</a>, which covers the lily-versus-poinsettia confusion in more detail, and our post on <a href="/blog/is-christmas-cactus-poisonous-to-cats/">whether Christmas cactus is poisonous to cats</a> for a comparison of which holiday plants are actually safe.</p>

<h2>Other Common Houseplants, Checked Against the ASPCA List</h2>

<p>Below each plant is what the ASPCA's plant database actually says, not a guess.</p>

<h3>Sago palm</h3>
<p>Toxic, and seriously so. The toxic principle is cycasin, and the ASPCA lists effects ranging from vomiting and black, tarry stool to liver damage, liver failure, and death. Sago palm is one of the few houseplants where a vet visit should happen immediately after any suspected chewing, not after symptoms appear.</p>

<h3>Pothos (Devil's Ivy)</h3>
<p>Toxic. Pothos contains insoluble calcium oxalate crystals, which cause oral irritation, swelling and pain in the mouth, tongue and lips, drooling, vomiting, and difficulty swallowing. It's one of the most popular low-maintenance houseplants around, which is exactly why it ends up on so many "is this safe" searches.</p>

<h3>Philodendron</h3>
<p>Toxic, across essentially every common variety the ASPCA lists separately (heartleaf, split-leaf, tree philodendron, and variegated types). The mechanism is the same calcium oxalate irritation seen in pothos: burning in the mouth, drooling, vomiting, and trouble swallowing.</p>

<h3>Dieffenbachia (dumb cane)</h3>
<p>Toxic. Along with calcium oxalates, dieffenbachia contains a proteolytic enzyme, and the ASPCA lists the same family of symptoms, intense oral irritation, drooling, vomiting, and difficulty swallowing, as its calcium-oxalate relatives.</p>

<h3>Aloe vera</h3>
<p>Toxic, which surprises a lot of people since the gel is often used on human skin and even eaten in small amounts. The ASPCA lists aloe as toxic to cats regardless, with vomiting, lethargy, and diarrhea as the clinical signs.</p>

<h3>ZZ plant</h3>
<p>This one doesn't have a dedicated, detailed public ASPCA entry the way the others do, so it's worth being straightforward about the uncertainty: multiple veterinary and poison-control sources describe ZZ plant as containing calcium oxalate crystals similar to pothos and dieffenbachia, causing oral irritation and vomiting if chewed, generally without the more serious systemic effects seen in sago palm or lilies. Treat it as a "keep it out of reach" plant rather than a harmless one, and call your vet or ASPCA Poison Control if your cat chews on it.</p>

